Repository: sindresorhus/p-memoize
Issue: A synchronous cache.has exception permanently caches the rejected promise for that key

A custom cache may implement `has()` synchronously. If it throws once, every subsequent call with that key returns the original rejected promise without consulting the cache again, even after the cache recovers.

Reproduced on Node.js v22.23.3 with main at `e0912bb5364c8421e189f018501a474d502f64cb` (package version 8.0.0). I compiled `index.ts` locally with TypeScript and ran this against the emitted module:

```js
import pMemoize from 'p-memoize';

let lookups = 0;
const cache = {
    has() {
        if (++lookups === 1) {
            throw new Error('temporary cache outage');
        }
        return false;
    },
    get() {},
    set() {},
    delete() {},
};

const memoized = pMemoize(async key => `ok:${key}`, {cache});

for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 3; attempt++) {
    try {
        console.log(await memoized('key'));
    } catch (error) {
        console.log(error.message);
    }
}
console.log({lookups});
```

Expected: one failure, followed by successful retries. The cache should be checked again after the rejected attempt.

Actual:

```text
temporary cache outage
temporary cache outage
temporary cache outage
{ lookups: 1 }
```

The async IIFE evaluates `cache.has(key)` before reaching its first suspension. If that call throws synchronously, its `finally` deletes the key before the outer `promiseCache.set(key, promise)` has run. That outer `set` then stores an already-rejected promise with no remaining cleanup.

Deferring the IIFE body until a microtask (so the promise is registered before any cache access) fixes this focused regression in a local probe. An asynchronously rejected `has()` does not have the same ordering problem. I only ran the focused probe, not the full project suite.

